<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  WinSW configuration for the NEW (live-memory) CowAgent backend.

  Run the backend as a Windows service on port 9902, ALONGSIDE the old
  desktop app on 9876. Both run at the same time; they share the same
  memory store but use different readers.

  Naming rule (WinSW requirement): the XML file name MUST match the
  executable base name. The exe is "CowAgentBackend.exe", so the config
  is "CowAgentBackend.xml" - WinSW discovers it by convention.

  WHY THIS SERVICE EXISTS

  A backend started from an agent session dies with the session: the
  process tree is reaped when the parent exits, and - worse - the
  desktop app's own update/restart flow kills whatever PID sits in
  <project_root>\\.cow.pid, which a second instance in the same source
  tree claims for itself. A service has its own process, its own
  lifetime, and a supervisor that restarts it when it dies.

  WHY THE ENVIRONMENT IS SET HERE AND NOT IN THE BATCH FILE

  The service account matters more than usual. The memory store lives at
  C:\\Users\\User\\cow and the config at C:\\Users\\User\\.cow - both under the
  interactive user profile. Running as LocalSystem would point HOME and
  USERPROFILE at C:\\Windows\\System32\\config\\systemprofile, and the
  backend would come up against an EMPTY workspace: healthy, serving,
  and remembering nothing. That is the same silent failure as the old
  reader, so the account is pinned explicitly below.

  The four settings that decide whether the service can read the real
  history at all:

    AGENT_WORKSPACE = C:\\Users\\User\\cow
        config.py overrides config.json with this. If it points at the
        source tree, state_dir.assert_not_source_tree() REFUSES to write
        memory/long-term/index.db and the backend starts against an
        empty store. It must be the workspace directory.

    COW_WEB_PORT = 9902
        channel/web/core/channel.py:1538 reads this, falling back to
        conf()["web_port"] and then 9899. The old desktop backend owns
        9876, so a fixed port avoids the collision.

    COW_DATA_DIR = C:\\Users\\User\\.cow
        Where config.json and run.log live.

    COW_DESKTOP is deliberately NOT set.
        Setting it to 1 turns on desktop mode (no browser pop-up, IM
        plugin denylist, tighter "workspace-write" permission). This is
        a plain web console.

  SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH

  The service does NOT carry its own copy of the startup logic. It runs
  cmd.exe with start-backend.cmd, which is exactly what a person
  double-clicking that file runs. Change the launcher, and the manual
  start and the service change together.
-->
<service>
  <id>CowAgentBackend</id>
  <name>CowAgent Backend (live-memory)</name>
  <description>New CowAgent web backend on port 9902. Reads the externalised memory store (content_path / content_state) so the full history is visible. Runs alongside the old desktop app on 9876.</description>

  <!--
    Run the batch launcher through cmd.exe, not python directly.

    cmd.exe is the executable: WinSW monitors THAT process, and cmd.exe
    stays in the foreground for as long as the server runs. Launching
    python directly would work too, but then the service and the manual
    start are two different code paths again, and they drift.

    The launcher runs the backend in the foreground (it does not detach),
    so WinSW's child IS the server.
  -->
  <executable>C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe</executable>
  <arguments>/c "C:\\Workspace\\AI-Agent\\CowAgent\\deploy\\start-backend-service.cmd"</arguments>
  <workingdirectory>C:\\Workspace\\AI-Agent\\CowAgent\\deploy</workingdirectory>

  <!--
    Run as LocalSystem, like the existing CowReranker service.

    WHY NOT THE INTERACTIVE USER
    A service running as "vm-win11\\user" needs that account's PASSWORD at
    install time. The XML previously carried the literal placeholder
    "will be supplied at install time" - installing it as-is would set that
    string AS the password, and the service would fail to start with a
    logon error nobody could act on.

    A password must not travel through a chat transcript: that is the
    exact leak pattern the F-5 review closed
    (knowledge/engineering/public-key-ssh-eliminates-password-passing.md).

    WHY LocalSystem IS SAFE HERE
    Every path the backend touches is an ABSOLUTE path set in the <env>
    blocks below - nothing resolves through "~" or %USERPROFILE%. And
    LocalSystem CAN read/write the memory store; verified with icacls:
        C:\\Users\\User\\cow      SYSTEM:(I)(OI)(CI)(F)
        C:\\Users\\User\\.cow     SYSTEM:(I)(OI)(CI)(F)
    (F) = full control, inherited.

    The one thing LocalSystem gets wrong by default is %USERPROFILE%
    (it points at C:\\Windows\\System32\\config\\systemprofile), which would
    make expanduser("~") resolve to the systemprofile and the backend
    would come up against an EMPTY workspace: healthy, serving, and
    remembering nothing. The <env> block pins USERPROFILE to the real
    profile, so "~" resolves exactly as it does interactively.
  -->
  <env name="USERPROFILE" value="C:\\Users\\User" />
  <env name="HOME" value="C:\\Users\\User" />
  <!--
    APPDATA / LOCALAPPDATA ARE NOT REDUNDANT WITH USERPROFILE.

    Python derives the USER site-packages directory from %APPDATA%, not from
    %USERPROFILE%:

        site.getusersitepackages()
          -> %APPDATA%\\Python\\PythonXY\\site-packages

    Under LocalSystem, %APPDATA% is
    C:\\Windows\\System32\\config\\systemprofile\\AppData\\Roaming - so Python
    looked for the third-party packages in the systemprofile and found
    NOTHING. yaml / requests / web were all missing, the web channel
    failed to construct, and the service came up RUNNING with no socket
    bound at all: no LISTENING on 9902, and the browser saw a blank page.

    Pinning USERPROFILE alone is not enough - that was the bug in the
    previous revision of this file. The three packages the backend needs
    are installed in the USER's site-packages
    (C:\\Users\\User\\AppData\\Roaming\\Python\\Python315\\site-packages), so
    APPDATA has to be pinned too. Measured: with APPDATA + LOCALAPPDATA
    pinned, site.getusersitepackages() returns the real path and
    yaml/requests/web all import.
  -->
  <env name="APPDATA" value="C:\\Users\\User\\AppData\\Roaming" />
  <env name="LOCALAPPDATA" value="C:\\Users\\User\\AppData\\Local" />
  <env name="AGENT_WORKSPACE" value="C:\\Users\\User\\cow" />
  <env name="COW_WEB_PORT" value="9902" />
  <env name="COW_DATA_DIR" value="C:\\Users\\User\\.cow" />

  <serviceaccount>
    <user>LocalSystem</user>
    <allowinteractive>true</allowinteractive>
  </serviceaccount>

  <!--
    Start mode: Automatic, delayed. Delayed start means this service
    starts AFTER the network stack and other auto-start services, which
    keeps the backend's cold start (it loads 15 agent profiles and the
    scheduler) from competing with the user logon.

    The old desktop app is NOT a dependency: the two must be able to run
    independently, and one being down must not take the other with it.
  -->
  <startmode>Automatic</startmode>
  <delayedAutoStart />

  <!--
    Restart on failure.

    The backend exits non-zero on an unrecoverable startup error. WinSW
    reports SERVICE_STOPPED on exit code 0, and the SCM treats that as
    a clean stop and never applies the failure actions - so the service
    would stay down until someone noticed. The launcher's foreground
    mode normalises a zero exit code to 1 for that reason.

    A deliberate "sc stop" is unaffected: DoStop() sets the
    orderlyShutdown flag, and that branch never looks at the exit code.
  -->
  <onfailure action="restart" delay="10 sec" />
  <onfailure action="restart" delay="30 sec" />
  <onfailure action="restart" delay="60 sec" />
  <resetfailure>1 hour</resetfailure>

  <!--
    Graceful shutdown: the backend drains in-flight requests on SIGTERM.
    WinSW sends Ctrl+C first, then escalates to TerminateProcess after
    stoptimeout. 15s is enough for the scheduler to checkpoint.
  -->
  <stoptimeout>15 sec</stoptimeout>
  <stopparentprocessfirst>true</stopparentprocessfirst>

  <priority>Normal</priority>

  <!--
    Logs: roll-by-size so the service cannot fill the disk over months.
    keepFiles=5 at 10MB each = 50MB ceiling per log stream.
  -->
  <logpath>C:\\Users\\User\\cow\\backend-logs</logpath>
  <log mode="roll-by-size">
    <sizeThreshold>10240</sizeThreshold>
    <keepFiles>5</keepFiles>
  </log>
</service>
